he starter `.ard/` tree locally. Commit it on a branch and merge\n42\t+it with **Squash** or a **merge commit**, never fast-forward or rebase (see the\n43\t+`ard init` lane in [Guardrails](../proposal.md#guardrails)). Nothing takes\n44\t+effect until it is merged, because `ard serve` reads `origin/main`. Use\n45\t+`ard init --pr` to have ard open the `ard-init` PR.\n46\t \n47\t-## 3. Sync labels\n48\t+## 3. Run setup\n49\t \n50\t ```sh\n51\t-ard labels sync\n52\t+ard setup --repo <owner>/<name>\n53\t ```\n54\t \n55\t-## 4. Branch protection on `main`\n56\t+This creates the labels and checks permissions, the ruleset and App bypass, App\n57\t+permissions and merge settings. `ard setup --check` only reports and exits 2 on\n58\t+drift.\n59\t \n60\t-Protect `main` with a ruleset and put the App on its bypass list (mode Always).\n61\t-The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main`; see \"Branch\n62\t-protection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n63\t+## 4. Fix the manual rows\n64\t \n65\t-## 5. Copy the bot-commit audit\n66\t+Each `manual: <name>: <fix>` row carries the exact fix. Typical ones:\n67\t \n68\t-By hand, in the target repository (do not edit `.github/` here): copy\n69\t-`.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set the\n70\t-repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n71\t+- `ruleset`: protect `main` with a ruleset and put the App on its bypass list\n72\t+  (mode Always). The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to\n73\t+  `main`; see \"Branch protection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n74\t+- `merge-settings`: turn off rebase merging.\n75\t+- Bot-commit audit, by hand in the target repository (do not edit `.github/`\n76\t+  here): copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set\n77\t+  the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n78\t \n79\t-## 6. Set this serve's limits\n80\t+Rerun `ard setup` until it is green.\n81\t \n82\t+## 5. Set this serve's limits\n83\t `max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` in `.ard/config.yaml` apply\n84\t to this `ard serve` alone. When a host or cluster runs more than one, set each\n85\t serve's limits so the **total** fits the host, the cluster and the model\n86\t@@ -67,7 +74,7 @@ invent one. Divide the subscription's token allowance through `max_pods` and the\n87\t dollar caps above. Each serve records a usage limit on its own, so one serve\n88\t hitting the subscription limit does not stop the other until it hits it too.\n89\t \n90\t-## 7. Dry run, soak, go live\n91\t+## 6. Dry run, soak, go live\n92\t \n93\t ```sh\n94\t ard serve --dry-run\n95\t@@ -82,7 +89,7 @@ deployment repository (check the key name in that repository's docs). Live agent\n96\t jobs in the cluster need #54; until it lands, run the cluster instance in\n97\t dry-run only.\n98\t \n99\t-## 8. Optional: a shared roles repository\n100\t+## 7. Optional: a shared roles repository\n101\t \n102\t `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads\n103\t roles from a shared repository, and the repository's own role of the same name\n104\tdiff --git a/docs/site/getting-started.md b/docs/site/getting-started.md\n105\tindex ffb026f..81c2107 100644\n106\t--- a/docs/site/getting-started.md\n107\t+++ b/docs/site/getting-started.md\n108\t@@ -30,34 +30,43 @@ Do **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edi\n109\t \n110\t Give `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n111\t \n112\t-## 2. Let the App push to main\n113\t+## 2. Initialise the repository\n114\t \n115\t-ard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n116\t+```sh\n117\t+ard init\n118\t+```\n119\t \n120\t-## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n121\t+`ard init` writes the starter `.ard/` tree into your local clone: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review the files, commit them on a branch and merge them by pull request. Merge with **Squash** or a **merge commit**, never fast-forward or rebase. To have ard open the pull request for you, run `ard init --pr` instead; it opens the `ard-init` PR.\n122\t \n123\t-Set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n124\t+Nothing takes effect until the tree is merged, because `ard serve` reads its configuration from `origin/main`. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes.\n125\t \n126\t-## 4. Initialise the repository\n127\t+## 3. Run setup\n128\t \n129\t ```sh\n130\t-ard init\n131\t+ard setup\n132\t ```\n133\t \n134\t-`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review and merge it. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes. Then:\n135\t+`ard setup` creates the `ard:` labels (it never deletes one) and checks the App's permissions, the ruleset on `main` and the App's bypass, the App permissions (no `workflows` or administration) and the merge settings. It prints one row per check: `permissions`, `labels`, `ruleset`, `app-permissions` and `merge-settings`, each with the status ok, changed, drift or manual.\n136\t \n137\t-```sh\n138\t-ard labels sync\n139\t-ard config check\n140\t-```\n141\t+To report without changing anything, run `ard setup --check`. It exits with code 2 when anything has drifted.\n142\t+\n143\t+## 4. Fix any manual rows\n144\t+\n145\t+A row that setup cannot fix or read for itself prints as `manual: <name>: <fix>`. The text after the name is the exact fix, so do what it says. The usual ones:\n146\t+\n147\t+- `ruleset`: if `main` has a ruleset, it must require a pull request and let only the ard App bypass it. ard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`, and without the bypass those pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n148\t+- `app-permissions`: remove any permission the row names from the App.\n149\t+- `merge-settings`: turn off rebase merging, as the row says.\n150\t+\n151\t+Also audit the bot's commits. Set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n152\t \n153\t-`ard labels sync` creates the `ard:` labels and never deletes one. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n154\t+Rerun `ard setup` until no row is drift or manual. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n155\t \n156\t ## 5. Store the model credential\n157\t \n158\t Set `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a subscription token from `claude setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the environment of `ard serve`. By default the token wins when both are set; `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` set to `oauth` or `apikey` forces one. The value is passed to the agent job and is never logged. If you use a subscription, turn off extra usage in the account so a usage limit is not billed as overage.\n159\t \n160\t-## 6. Start serving\n161\t+## 6. Dry run, then go live\n162\t \n163\t Try a dry run first. It logs every side effect without performing it:\n164\t \n165\tdiff --git a/docs/site/index.md b/docs/site/index.md\n166\tindex e443979..bba89f2 100644\n167\t--- a/docs/site/index.md\n168\t+++ b/docs/site/index.md\n169\t@@ -15,7 +15,7 @@ ard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. One `ard` process per repository\n170\t \n171\t ## The `ard:` labels\n172\t \n173\t-GitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`, for example `ard:triage:lead:requested`. Run `ard labels sync` to create them. The ones you will touch:\n174\t+GitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`, for example `ard:triage:lead:requested`. Run `ard setup` to create them. The ones you will touch:\n175\t \n176\t | Label | Meaning |\n177\t | --- | --- |\n178\tdiff --git a/docs/site/troubleshooting.md b/docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n179\tindex 731f9ef..f61de40 100644\n180\t--- a/docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n181\t+++ b/docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n182\t@@ -34,4 +34,4 @@ The worker could not get `tests` green within `max_test_fixes` rounds. Read the\n183\t \n184\t ## Labels are missing or stale\n185\t \n186\t-Run `ard labels sync`. It creates and updates labels and reports stale ones, but never deletes them.\n187\t+Run `ard setup`. It creates and updates labels and reports stale ones, but never deletes them.\n188\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"346cb857-0330-4812-9aed-452c3e4a24eb","uuid":"1a0d617c-885d-4dad-bb64-5a30cf554450","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:05:51.834Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/work/.ard-input/349/diff.patch","content":"UNTRUSTED INPUT: the change under review, written